WN.lb(49,{"mt_S9SKah-yi_":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The bowl-shaped hole at the top is the…","choices":["crater","river","fridge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the top of the volcano.","explain":"The crater is the bowl at the top around the vent."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"An eruption is when…","choices":["magma, ash or gas bursts out","a toy falls over","rain makes a puddle"],"answer":0,"hint":"What comes out of the vent suddenly?","explain":"An eruption is magma, ash or gas bursting out of the volcano."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ash from a volcano is…","choices":["fine dusty rock from the eruption","shelf dust from cleaning","melted chocolate"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ash is made when rock is blasted into fine bits.","explain":"Volcanic ash is fine rock from the eruption, not ordinary house dust."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which sentence is true?","choices":["magma is under the ground; lava is on the surface","lava stays under the ground forever","magma only exists in cartoons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Name changes when melted rock comes out.","explain":"Magma is underground. On the surface the same melted rock is called lava."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A model shows {n} volcano vents. How many vents?","answer":"n","hint":"Count the vents on the model.","explain":"The model shows {n} vents — holes where magma can come out.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} slopes each show {k} lava flows. How many lava flows in all?","answer":"n*k","hint":"Equal groups: {n} slopes of {k}.","explain":"{n} × {k} = {=n*k} lava flows in all.","params":{"n":[2,3],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Every tall mountain…","choices":["is not a volcano unless it has a vent for melted rock","is always erupting","is made of plastic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Volcanoes need a vent linked to magma.","explain":"Only mountains with a vent for magma are volcanoes. Height alone is not enough."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why cool lava turns to hard rock?","choices":["it loses heat and solidifies","it turns into rain","it becomes a plastic toy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hot melted rock hardens when it cools.","explain":"Lava cools, loses heat, and becomes solid rock. It does not become rain or plastic."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A volcano is…","choices":["a mountain with a hole for hot melted rock","any tall hill","a plastic toy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about a vent where melted rock can come out.","explain":"A volcano has a vent where magma can erupt. Ordinary hills and toys are not volcanoes."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Magma is hot melted rock…","choices":["still under the ground","only in the sky","made of plastic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is the melted rock before it erupts?","explain":"Magma is under the ground. Once it flows out it is called lava."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A diagram labels {n} magma spots and {k} lava spots. How many magma spots?","answer":"n","hint":"Count only the magma labels.","explain":"There are {n} magma spots. The {k} lava spots are separate.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"An eruption sends out {n} ash clouds. {k} have already blown away. How many are left to watch?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the ones that blew away.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} ash clouds left.","params":{"n":[4,7],"k":[1,3]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Lava is…","choices":["magma that has reached the surface","cold rainwater","the name for any mountain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lava is what we call melted rock after it comes out.","explain":"When magma reaches the surface it is called lava. Rain and ordinary mountains are not lava."}],"explain":{"kid":["A volcano is a special mountain. It has a hole where hot melted rock can come out.","Magma is hot melted rock still under the ground. When it comes out, we call it lava.","An eruption is when magma, ash or gas bursts out of the volcano.","The crater is the bowl-shaped hole at the top. Ash is fine dusty rock that can shoot into the air."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7 earth science: volcano, vent, crater, magma, lava, eruption, ash. Magma stays under the ground. Lava is magma that has reached the surface.","Common mistakes: calling all hot rock lava even when it is still under the ground; thinking every mountain is a volcano; saying volcanoes are only fire or smoke with no melted rock; mixing ash with ordinary dust from a shelf.","In the lab your child builds a simple cross-section, fills the pipe with magma, erupts lava down the slope, labels the crater and ash, sorts magma from lava, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Look at a picture of a volcano together. Point to the crater at the top and ask: 'Is the hot rock still under the ground (magma) or on the surface (lava)?'","keyIdea":"A volcano is a mountain or hill with a hole (vent) where hot melted rock from under the ground can come out. Melted rock under the ground is magma. When it flows out it is called lava. An eruption is when magma, ash or gas bursts out.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Magma, lava, or neither? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Magma","Lava","Neither"],"items":[["hot melted rock still under the ground",0],["hot melted rock flowing on the surface",1],["cold river water",2],["melted rock inside the volcano pipe",0],["red rock cooling on the slope",1],["a plastic toy mountain",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagvolc","caption":"Flagship lab: build a volcano cutaway, fill magma, erupt lava, label crater and ash, sort magma vs lava, and catch mix-ups.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_e29VrLfmYt":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Sudden ground shake is…","choices":["an earthquake clue","volcano ash","ordinary wind"],"answer":0,"hint":"A sudden shake points to a quake, not ash or wind.","explain":"Sudden shake is a quake clue; ash points to a volcano."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"First safety move?","choices":["Drop low","Run to a window","Stand under a shelf"],"answer":0,"hint":"Get your body low first — do not run toward glass.","explain":"Drop low, then Cover and Hold — avoid glass and shelves."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In a real quake the ground…","choices":["shakes; forever holes are a myth","always opens a bottomless canyon","turns into water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cartoons exaggerate cracks; real quakes mainly shake.","explain":"Shaking is real; forever cartoon canyons are not typical."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Plates are…","choices":["pieces of Earth's shell","only puddles","only clouds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Earth's outer shell cracked into big pieces.","explain":"Plates are pieces of Earth's outer crust."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Safety steps in Drop-Cover-Hold (still 3 after {n} drills) = ?","answer":"3","params":{"n":[2,5]},"hint":"Drop, Cover, and Hold remain three named moves every time.","explain":"After {n} drills the plan still has 3 steps."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"If you add {k} plate cracks (each ≤4), cracks shown = ?","answer":"k","hint":"Each add within one to four shows that many cracks on the shell.","explain":"Adding {k} cracks shows {k} cracks on the shell.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Drop-Cover-Hold means…","choices":["get low, cover, hold on","jump higher each time","stand by glass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stay protected under sturdy cover until shaking stops.","explain":"Drop low, Cover under sturdy cover, Hold on."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: explain a quake simply.","items":["Earth's shell has plates","plates can bump or slide","the ground may shake"],"hint":"Name the shell plates, then motion, then the shake.","explain":"Plates move; that motion can shake the ground — an earthquake."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An earthquake is…","choices":["the ground shaking","only loud thunder","only heavy rain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the ground does in an earthquake.","explain":"An earthquake means the ground shakes when plates move."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Earthquakes are caused by…","choices":["plates moving","clouds bumping","the Moon falling"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look under the ground for moving plates, not the sky.","explain":"Moving plates bump or slide and can shake the ground."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Drop-Cover-Hold steps stay 3 even if you practise {n} times — type 3.","answer":"3","params":{"n":[1,4]},"hint":"The plan always has three named moves no matter the practice count.","explain":"Practising {n} times still leaves 3 steps: Drop, Cover, Hold."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"If you tap Shake {n} times in the lab, shakes logged = ?","answer":"n","hint":"Each Shake tap in the lab adds one shake to the count.","explain":"{n} taps mean {=n} shakes logged.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order the safety steps.","items":["Drop low","Cover under a table","Hold on"],"hint":"Get your body low before you cover and hold on.","explain":"Drop, then Cover, then Hold until shaking stops."}],"explain":{"kid":["Earth's outer shell is cracked into big pieces called plates.","When plates bump or slide, the ground can shake.","That shaking is an earthquake.","If it shakes, Drop low, Cover your head, and Hold on."],"adult":["KS1 rocks/earth & hazards: earthquakes as ground shaking from plate motion (simple plate idea). Words: plate, crust, shake, Drop Cover Hold. Distinct from volcanoes (magma/ash).","Common mistakes: thinking quakes are only thunder or rain; cartoon forever canyons; running to windows; mixing volcano ash with quake shake.","In the lab your child adds plate cracks, shakes the ground, picks the true cause, sorts clues, practises Drop-Cover-Hold, and rejects canyon myths."]},"familyTip":"Shake a jelly on a plate to show ground motion. Practise Drop-Cover-Hold under a sturdy table. Contrast with volcano ash/lava pictures.","keyIdea":"An earthquake is the ground shaking when pieces of Earth's crust (plates) bump, scrape, or slide. Stay safe with Drop, Cover, Hold.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Earthquake idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["plates bumping can shake the ground",0],["Drop, Cover, Hold is a safety plan",0],["sudden ground shake is a quake clue",0],["earth always opens a forever canyon",1],["earthquakes are only loud thunder",1],["Earth's shell is cracked into plates",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagearthq","caption":"Flagship lab: crack the crust into plates, shake the ground, name the cause, sort quake clues, practise Drop-Cover-Hold, reject canyon myths, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_adpdEaKEBC":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Erosion by wind and water is…","choices":["slow change","the same as a quake","not a real change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Erosion is the wearing away of rock over time.","explain":"Erosion is slow change. Tiny wears add up over years."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which pair is both FAST?","choices":["eruption and earthquake","wind wear and river rounding","one drip and one gust"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fast means seconds or minutes, not years.","explain":"Eruptions and earthquakes reshape land quickly. Wind and river wear are slow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Wind wears {a} mm each month. How many mm in {b} months?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Slow change adds the same small amount each month.","explain":"{a} mm per month times {b} months is {=a*b} mm. Tiny wears add up.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Slow change means…","choices":["change that takes a long time","no change ever happens","only changes on other planets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Slow is about timescale, not about 'never'.","explain":"Slow change is still real. It just takes years to notice."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A landslide that falls in minutes is…","choices":["a fast change","erosion over centuries","not an Earth change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Minutes is a short timescale.","explain":"A landslide can move a huge mass of land quickly. That is fast."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Ice widens a crack by {a} mm each winter. After {b} winters, how many mm wide is the new growth?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply mm per winter by the number of winters.","explain":"{a} mm each winter for {b} winters makes {=a*b} mm of new crack — slow change.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is the best evidence of slow change?","choices":["a cliff worn smoother over years","a crack that appears during a shake","lava that covers a road in an hour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Slow evidence builds up over a long time.","explain":"A cliff worn smoother over years shows slow erosion. Cracks and lava are fast."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Calling every loud bang 'erosion' is a mix-up because…","choices":["erosion is slow wear, not a sudden bang","erosion never happens on Earth","only volcanoes cause erosion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Erosion is the slow wearing of rock by wind, water or ice.","explain":"Erosion is slow. Sudden bangs are usually quakes, blasts or collapses — fast events."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A volcanic eruption is…","choices":["a fast change","a slow change","not a change at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how long an eruption takes to crack and cover land.","explain":"An eruption can change the land in minutes. That timescale is a fast Earth change."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Wind wearing a cliff away is…","choices":["slow change (erosion)","a kind of earthquake","finished in one gust"],"answer":0,"hint":"One gust moves little dust. Years of wind move a lot of rock.","explain":"Wind wear needs many years. That slow wearing of rock is erosion."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"An earthquake changes the land…","choices":["in seconds","only over centuries","never"],"answer":0,"hint":"A quake is a sudden shake of the ground.","explain":"The ground can crack and slip during the shake. That is a fast change."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A cliff loses {a} cm of rock each year to wind. How many cm after {b} years?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Slow wear adds a little each year. Multiply cm per year by years.","explain":"{a} cm each year for {b} years is {=a*b} cm worn away. That adds up slowly.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A quake lasts about {a} seconds. A drip-wear path needs {b} years. How many more years is the slow change?","answer":"b","hint":"The slow change is measured in years. The quake seconds are not years.","explain":"The slow drip-wear takes {b} years. The quake's {a} seconds are a fast change on a different scale.","params":{"a":[5,20],"b":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["The ground under our feet can change. Some changes are fast. Some changes are slow.","A volcano erupting or an earthquake shaking can change the land in seconds or minutes. That is a fast change.","Wind and water rub tiny bits off rock. One rub is tiny. Many years of rubs wear a cliff or round a stone. That slow wear is called erosion.","Mountains and cliffs do change. Slow change is still real change — it just takes a long time to see."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. Builds toward NGSS 2-ESS1-1 (use information from several sources to provide evidence that Earth events can occur quickly or slowly) and KS1 ideas about rocks, soils and everyday materials shaped by weather. Prior topics: What Is a Volcano and What Is an Earthquake (soft prerequisites).","Fast Earth-surface changes include volcanic eruptions, earthquakes and many landslides. Slow changes include weathering and erosion by wind, water and ice. Both reshape the land; the difference is timescale, not whether change happens.","Common mistakes: thinking mountains never change; calling every loud event 'erosion'; thinking slow means 'nothing happens'; and mixing up the volcano or quake itself with the slow wearing of rock afterward.","In the lab your child sorts eruption, wind wear and quake into FAST or SLOW, scrubs a soft hill six times, shakes once for a quake, drips water eight times, and rejects the 'mountains never change' myth."]},"familyTip":"On a walk, look for a cracked pavement (fast damage after a freeze or dig) and a rounded river stone (slow wear). Ask: which change was quick, which took a long time?","keyIdea":"Earth's surface changes in two speeds: fast changes like volcanic eruptions and earthquakes happen in seconds or minutes, and slow changes like wind and water wearing rock away (erosion) take years.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fast or slow? Drag each Earth change into its speed box, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["fast","slow"],"items":[["volcano erupts",0],["wind wears a cliff",1],["earthquake shakes",0],["river rounds a stone",1],["landslide after heavy rain",0],["ice cracks rock over winters",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfastslow","caption":"Flagship lab: sort fast vs slow, scrub soft rock, one quake shake, drip-wear, catch 'mountains never change'.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_bhwf_rDXQL":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the Ring of Fire?","choices":["A horseshoe of volcanoes and earthquakes round the Pacific","A ring of real fire on the sea","A hot desert"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a name for a pattern on the map.","explain":"The Ring of Fire is the long line of volcanoes and earthquakes round the edge of the Pacific Ocean."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which ocean does the Ring of Fire go round?","choices":["The Pacific Ocean","The Atlantic Ocean","The Arctic Ocean"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the biggest ocean, in the middle of the lab map.","explain":"The ring goes round the edge of the Pacific, the biggest ocean."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A plate creeps about 5 cm each year. How far does it move in {y} years (cm)?","answer":"5*y","hint":"Each year adds 5 cm. Count in 5s, or multiply.","explain":"{y} × 5 = {=5*y} cm. Plates are slow: that is about the length of a hand for every few years.","params":{"y":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Some volcanoes are split into {g} groups of 4. In each group, 3 are on the ring. How many volcanoes are on the ring?","answer":"3*g","hint":"Each group of 4 gives 3 on the ring. Count in 3s, or multiply.","explain":"{g} × 3 = {=3*g}, so {=3*g} of the {=4*g} volcanoes are on the ring.","params":{"g":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Earth's rocky outside is cut into huge pieces. What are they called?","choices":["Plates","Rings","Craters"],"answer":0,"hint":"The dashed lines on the map were their edges.","explain":"The pieces are called plates. They move slowly, and earthquakes happen at their edges."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which place is not on the Ring of Fire?","choices":["Brazil","Japan","New Zealand"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is far from the Pacific edge?","explain":"Brazil is in the middle of a plate, far from the Pacific edge. Japan and New Zealand are on the ring."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Alaska is cold. Is it on the Ring of Fire?","choices":["Yes, it is at a plate edge by the Pacific","No, only hot places are","No, it is too far north"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does heat from the Sun make earthquakes?","explain":"Alaska is on the ring. Earthquakes come from plates moving, not from hot weather."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ella says earthquakes can happen anywhere just as often. What is right?","choices":["Most happen in lines at plate edges","Ella is right","They only happen in cities"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember the map: were the dots spread out evenly?","explain":"The map shows lines, not an even spread. Most quakes are at plate edges."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"How fast do Earth's plates move?","choices":["Very slowly, about as fast as fingernails grow","As fast as a car","They never move"],"answer":0,"hint":"You cannot feel them moving day to day.","explain":"Plates move a few centimetres a year, about as fast as fingernails grow."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where do most big earthquakes happen?","choices":["In lines round the edge of the Pacific Ocean","Evenly all over the world","In the middle of big countries"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the map with all the red dots.","explain":"Most big earthquakes line up round the Pacific Ocean, in the Ring of Fire."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A map shows {n} groups of 10 big earthquakes. In each group of 10, about 8 are on the Ring of Fire. How many are on the ring?","answer":"8*n","hint":"Each group gives 8 quakes on the ring. Count in 8s, or multiply.","explain":"{n} × 8 = {=8*n}, so about {=8*n} of the {=10*n} quakes are on the ring.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why do earthquakes happen in lines?","choices":["They happen where Earth's plates meet","Hot weather makes the ground crack","Cities make the ground shake"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the dashed lines on the map show?","explain":"Earth's rocky outside is cut into plates. Earthquakes happen where plates meet and move."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Volcanoes are found in {a} places on one part of the ring and {b} on another. How many is that in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two numbers together.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} volcanoes.","params":{"a":[12,30],"b":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which place is on the Ring of Fire?","choices":["Chile","Brazil","The middle of Australia"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a place on the edge of the Pacific Ocean.","explain":"Chile is on the Pacific coast of South America, where two plates meet. Brazil and the middle of Australia are far from a plate edge."}],"explain":{"kid":["Earthquakes and volcanoes do not pop up just anywhere. If you mark every big earthquake on a map, the marks make lines. Volcanoes sit in the same lines.","The biggest line goes round the edge of the Pacific Ocean, like a giant horseshoe. It is called the Ring of Fire. It runs from New Zealand up past Indonesia, the Philippines and Japan, across to Alaska, and down the coast of the Americas to Chile.","Why there? Earth's rocky outside is cut into huge pieces called plates. They move very slowly, about as fast as your fingernails grow. Where two plates push, pull or slide past each other, the ground can shake. Rock can melt and come up as a volcano.","The Ring of Fire is not real fire. It is a name for all those volcanoes and earthquakes. Places in the middle of a plate, like the middle of Australia or Brazil, are far from an edge. Big earthquakes are rare there, and they have no active volcanoes."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. This matches NGSS 4-ESS2-2: analyse and interpret data from maps to describe patterns of Earth's features, including the locations of earthquakes, volcanoes and mountain ranges. In England it fits key stage 2 geography: describe and understand physical geography, including volcanoes and earthquakes. Key words: earthquake, volcano, pattern, Ring of Fire, plate, plate boundary or edge, Pacific Ocean, map.","The US Geological Survey says that about 81% of the world's largest earthquakes happen along the Ring of Fire. It is about 40,000 km long and has about 450 volcanoes, roughly three quarters of the world's active volcanoes. Most of the rest are in the Alpide belt, from the Mediterranean through Iran to the Himalaya, and along mid-ocean ridges. The lab map is a simple picture, not to scale, and its dots are drawn to match these real patterns.","Common mistakes: thinking earthquakes and volcanoes are random or spread evenly; thinking they happen in hot places, so the Sun or a hot climate causes them; thinking cities or people cause big earthquakes; taking the name literally, as a ring of real fire on the sea; and thinking the middle of a big country is just as likely to have a big quake.","In the lab your child drops 90 earthquakes and sees them fall in lines. They add volcanoes in the same lines and trace the ring with a finger. Then they tap quakes to see which two plates meet there, and check eight places to find the five that are on the ring."]},"familyTip":"Look at a world map or globe together and find the Pacific Ocean. Trace round its edge with a finger: New Zealand, Indonesia, the Philippines, Japan, Alaska, California, Mexico, Peru and Chile. Then look up the news for the last big earthquake and see if it was on your ring.","keyIdea":"Earthquakes and volcanoes do not happen just anywhere. Most of them happen in long lines, and the biggest line is a horseshoe round the edge of the Pacific Ocean called the Ring of Fire. It runs from New Zealand, up past Indonesia and Japan, across to Alaska and down the west coast of the Americas to Chile. These lines are where the giant plates that make up Earth's rocky outside meet and move.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each place on the Ring of Fire, round the Pacific edge, or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["On the Ring of Fire","Not on the ring"],"items":[["Japan",0],["Middle of Australia",1],["Chile",0],["Brazil",1],["New Zealand",0],["Madagascar",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrfire","caption":"Flagship lab: drop earthquakes and volcanoes on a map, trace the Ring of Fire, find the plate edges, then check which places are on it.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_mdZ3nBWChW":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"How many steps up the magnitude scale is a wiggle {=10**a} times taller?","answer":"a","hint":"Count the tens multiplied together to make that number.","explain":"{=10**a} is 10 multiplied together {a} times, and each ×10 is one step. So it is {a} steps up.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"At 100 km, a {=10**a} mm wiggle means magnitude {=3+a}. What magnitude makes a wiggle 10 times taller at the same place?","answer":"4+a","hint":"One ×10 in wiggle height is one step up the scale.","explain":"10 times taller is one step up: {=3+a} + 1 = {=4+a}.","params":{"a":[0,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word means how hard the ground shook in one place?","choices":["Intensity","Magnitude","Epicentre"],"answer":0,"hint":"One of these changes from town to town.","explain":"Intensity describes the shaking in one place, from IV (dishes rattle) to VIII (chimneys fall). Magnitude is one number for the whole quake."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why does the pen on a seismometer hang from a heavy weight?","choices":["The weight stays almost still while the ground moves","Heavy pens write darker lines","The weight makes the quake bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Something has to stay still for the pen to draw the movement.","explain":"A heavy weight hardly moves when the ground shakes. The paper moves with the ground under the still pen, so the pen draws the shaking."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Town A is 10 km from a quake. Town B is 200 km away on the same kind of rock. Which shook harder?","choices":["Town A","Town B","They shook the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Shaking gets weaker as the waves spread out.","explain":"Town A, closer to the epicentre, shook harder. Both towns had the same quake and magnitude, but different intensities."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Two towns are both 60 km from a quake. One sits on soft mud and one on hard rock. Which shakes more?","choices":["The one on soft mud","The one on hard rock","They shake the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about a bowl of jelly and a brick.","explain":"Soft ground such as mud shakes more strongly than hard rock, so its intensity is higher even at the same distance."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A magnitude 7 quake lets out about how much more energy than a magnitude 5?","choices":["About 1000 times","2 times","About 100 times"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each step is about 32 times the energy. There are two steps.","explain":"32 × 32 is about 1000, so a magnitude 7 lets out about 1000 times the energy. 100 times is the wiggle height for two steps, not the energy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Each year there are about 1,300 magnitude 5 quakes in the world. About how many magnitude 6 quakes are there?","choices":["About 130","About 13,000","About 1,300"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bigger quakes are rarer. How does the count change for each step up?","explain":"There are about 10 times fewer quakes for each step up, so about 130 magnitude 6 quakes a year (USGS counts about 134)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Ali says a magnitude 6 quake is twice as strong as a magnitude 3. Is he right?","choices":["No: 3 steps make the wiggle 1000 times taller","Yes: 6 is double 3","No: it is 3 times as strong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each step up the scale is ×10 in wiggle height.","explain":"From 3 to 6 is three steps of ×10, so the wiggle is 10 × 10 × 10 = 1000 times taller, and the energy is about 32,000 times more."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does a seismometer record?","choices":["How the ground shakes","How hot the rocks are","Where the plates meet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the wiggly line it draws.","explain":"A seismometer records ground shaking as a wiggly line called a seismogram. Taller wiggles mean the ground moved further."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"At 100 km, magnitude 3 makes a 1 mm wiggle. What magnitude makes a {=10**a} mm wiggle at 100 km?","answer":"3+a","hint":"Count how many times you multiply 1 mm by 10 to get there.","explain":"{=10**a} mm is 1 mm multiplied by 10 a total of {a} times. Each ×10 adds 1, so the magnitude is 3 + {a} = {=3+a}.","params":{"a":[0,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Quake P is magnitude {=b+a} and quake Q is magnitude {b}. At the same distance, how many times taller is the wiggle from P?","answer":"10**a","hint":"Each step up the scale makes the wiggle 10 times taller.","explain":"P is {a} steps above Q. Each step is ×10, so the wiggle is {=10**a} times taller.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[3,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A quake is magnitude 6.2. A town 300 km away barely feels it. What is the magnitude in that town?","choices":["6.2","About 2","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is magnitude about the whole quake, or about one place?","explain":"Magnitude is one number for the whole quake, so it is 6.2 everywhere. The far town has a low intensity, not a low magnitude."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How much more energy does a magnitude 6 quake let out than a magnitude 5?","choices":["About 32 times","About 1.2 times","Exactly 2 times"],"answer":0,"hint":"One step up the scale. Is it the ×10 wiggle rule or the energy rule?","explain":"Each step up lets out about 32 times as much energy. 1.2 comes from 6 ÷ 5, which treats magnitude as an ordinary number."}],"explain":{"kid":["A seismometer records shaking. A heavy weight hangs from a frame, with a pen on it. When the ground shakes, the frame and the paper move, but the heavy weight hardly moves. So the pen draws a wiggly line. Big shaking makes tall wiggles.","Magnitude tells you how big a quake is. Scientists measure the tallest wiggle. At 100 km away, a 1 mm wiggle is magnitude 3. A wiggle 10 times taller is magnitude 4. So magnitude 6 is not twice magnitude 3. Its wiggle is 1000 times taller.","Each step up also lets out about 32 times as much energy. Two steps is about 32 × 32, close to 1000. So one magnitude 7 quake lets out about as much energy as 1000 magnitude 5 quakes.","Intensity is how hard the ground shakes where you are. It is written in Roman numerals, like IV or VIII. Near the quake, chimneys may fall. Far away, dishes just rattle. Soft mud shakes more than hard rock. One quake has one magnitude but many intensities. Small quakes happen every day. Big ones are rare."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 11. NGSS 4-ESS2-2 uses maps of earthquake patterns and 4-ESS3-2 asks pupils to compare ways to reduce the effects of earthquakes; middle school MS-ESS3-2 adds forecasting from data. Measuring strength gives the words for both. A seismometer records ground motion, magnitude describes the size of the earthquake at its source, and intensity (the Modified Mercalli scale, I to XII) describes the effects at one place.","The lab moves from the instrument to the scales. Children shake a model seismometer and see the heavy pen stay still while the frame moves. They match wiggles of 1, 10 and 100 mm, all recorded 100 km away, to magnitudes 3, 4 and 5, which is how Richter first defined his scale. They turn a dial to see energy grow about 32 times a step, visit four towns round one magnitude 6.5 quake (the town intensities are a simple model), and place yearly USGS counts on magnitude bands.","Common mistakes. Children often treat magnitude as an ordinary number, so magnitude 6 seems twice magnitude 3. They mix up the ×10 wiggle with the ×32 energy step, or think the magnitude is lower in a far town. Many think the Richter scale measures damage, but damage is intensity and depends on distance, depth, ground and buildings. Scientists now use the moment magnitude scale for large quakes; its steps match, so these rules still hold. Ask: is this number about the whole quake, or about one place?"]},"familyTip":"When an earthquake is in the news, find its magnitude and read how it felt in different towns. Ask each other: which number is the same everywhere, and which one changes from place to place?","keyIdea":"A seismometer records how the ground shakes. Its heavy weight holds a pen almost still while the ground and the paper move, so the pen draws a wiggly line called a seismogram. Magnitude is one number for the size of a whole earthquake. Each step up the scale means the wiggle is 10 times taller and the quake lets out about 32 times as much energy, so a magnitude 7 lets out about 1000 times the energy of a magnitude 5. Intensity is different: it describes how hard the ground shook in one place. It gets weaker further away and stronger on soft ground. Small earthquakes are common; big ones are rare.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each card about magnitude, intensity or the seismometer? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Magnitude: the whole quake","Intensity: one place","Seismometer: the recorder"],"items":[["One number for each quake",0],["Up 1 when wiggles are 10 times taller",0],["About 32 times the energy each step",0],["Dishes rattle in my town",1],["Weaker far from the epicentre",1],["Stronger on soft mud",1],["A heavy weight that stays still",2],["Draws a seismogram",2],["Paper that moves with the ground",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagquake","caption":"Flagship lab: shake a seismometer, turn wiggle heights into magnitudes, step up the energy, visit towns round one quake, rank how often quakes happen.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XG4RqUIXm8":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In which year did Vesuvius bury Pompeii?","choices":["AD 79","1748","AD 2000"],"answer":0,"hint":"Was it in Roman times or when the diggers came?","explain":"Vesuvius erupted in AD 79, in Roman times. 1748 is when digging began, almost 1,700 years later."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Put these in order. Which happened first?","choices":["Pompeii was a busy Roman town","Diggers started to uncover Pompeii","Archaeologists study Pompeii today"],"answer":0,"hint":"The town had to exist before it could be buried and found.","explain":"First the busy Roman town, then the eruption in AD 79, then the digging from 1748, and study today."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the right order to make a plaster cast?","choices":["Find a hollow, pour in plaster, let it set, dig away the ash","Dig away the ash, pour in plaster, find a hollow, let it set","Pour in plaster, find a hollow, dig away the ash, let it set"],"answer":0,"hint":"You cannot fill a hollow you have not found yet.","explain":"Find the hollow, fill it with plaster, wait for it to set hard, then dig away the ash to see the shape."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A street in Pompeii has {n} houses on each side. How many houses are on the street?","answer":"n*2","hint":"There are two sides with the same number of houses.","explain":"{n} + {n} = {=n*2} houses. Archaeologists give every house a number so they can map the town.","params":{"n":[6,25]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A baker's oven was found with loaves of bread still inside. What does this tell us?","choices":["People bought bread from bakeries","Romans did not eat bread","The volcano baked the bread"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why would one oven be full of loaves?","explain":"A bakery made lots of loaves to sell. The 81 loaves found in one oven show bakeries fed the town."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why are there big stepping stones across Pompeii's streets?","choices":["Streets got wet and dirty, and carts drove between the stones","They were seats for people to rest on","They were left by the volcano"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about a street after rain with carts going by.","explain":"The stones let people cross without stepping in water and dirt, and cart wheels passed in the gaps between them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A plaster cast took {d} days to make: 1 day to pour, and the rest to dig. How many days were spent digging?","answer":"d-1","hint":"Take away the 1 day of pouring.","explain":"{d} − 1 = {=d-1} days of digging. Digging is slow and careful so the shape does not break.","params":{"d":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia says lava buried Pompeii. What is the best answer?","choices":["No: ash and pumice fell on it, then hot ash clouds covered it","Yes: lava flowed down and covered every house","No: the sea flooded it"],"answer":0,"hint":"If lava had flowed in, would bread and paintings have survived?","explain":"Lava would have burned and melted things. Ash covered the town instead, which is why so much was kept."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why can we learn so much about Roman life from Pompeii?","choices":["A whole town was buried at once and kept by the ash","It is the only Roman town ever built","The Romans left a guidebook"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes Pompeii different from towns that slowly fell down?","explain":"The ash froze a whole town in time, with homes, shops, food and writing left as they were on one day."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What buried the town of Pompeii?","choices":["Ash and pumice falling from the sky, then hot ash clouds","Rivers of red-hot lava","A huge wave from the sea"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what fell on the houses in the lab.","explain":"Pumice and ash fell for hours, then clouds of hot ash rushed over the town. No lava reached Pompeii."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In a model, ash piles up {n} centimetres every hour for 10 hours. How many centimetres of ash is that?","answer":"n*10","hint":"Multiply the amount each hour by 10 hours.","explain":"{n} × 10 = {=n*10} centimetres of ash. It kept piling up, so roofs fell in under the weight.","params":{"n":[5,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why did paintings and bread last so long at Pompeii?","choices":["The ash covered them and kept out air and rain","The Romans painted them with magic paint","People looked after them for 1,700 years"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the ash do, like a thick blanket?","explain":"The ash packed tight round everything and went hard, shutting out air, rain and people, so things kept their shape and colour."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"An archaeologist digs up {a} coins one day and {b} coins the next day. How many coins is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two days together.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} coins. Coins show which emperors ruled when the town was buried.","params":{"a":[12,40],"b":[5,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How did archaeologists see the shapes of people buried at Pompeii?","choices":["They poured plaster into hollows left in the ash","They found statues the Romans made","The people had turned into stone"],"answer":0,"hint":"What was left when the bodies rotted away?","explain":"The bodies rotted and left hollows in the hard ash. Plaster poured in set into their exact shapes."}],"explain":{"kid":["Long ago, Pompeii was a busy Roman town in Italy. It had shops, bakeries, baths and painted houses. Near the town was a big mountain called Vesuvius. The people did not know it was a volcano.","In AD 79 Vesuvius erupted. A huge cloud of ash and rock shot up into the sky. The wind blew it over Pompeii, and small stones called pumice and ash fell for hours. Many people ran away. The next morning, clouds of red-hot ash rushed down the mountain and covered the town. It was not lava that buried Pompeii. It was ash.","The ash packed tight round everything, like a thick blanket, and went hard. It kept out air and rain. So paintings kept their colours, and loaves of bread kept their shape. Some people and animals could not escape. Their bodies rotted away and left hollows in the ash. Later, people poured plaster into the hollows to see their shapes.","Pompeii stayed hidden under the ground for about 1,700 years. People forgot it was there. In 1748 diggers began to uncover it. Archaeologists are still digging today. The finds are clues that show how Romans lived: what they ate, how they wrote, and even that they kept guard dogs."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. This supports the National Curriculum in England for Year 3 rocks and KS2 history (the Roman Empire and its impact), and NGSS 4-ESS3-2 (natural hazards). It is a story topic with a science core: an explosive eruption buries a town in tephra rather than lava. Facts are checked against the Pompeii Archaeological Park, the British Museum, the Smithsonian Global Volcanism Program and USGS.","The lab has five tasks. Learners tap Vesuvius hour by hour and watch about 3 metres of pumice, then hot ash flows, bury the town. They drop five objects into the ash to see how each was kept. They make a plaster cast in Fiorelli's four steps. They order five story cards from AD 79 to today, and dig up four finds and match each to what it tells us.","Common mistakes. Most children think lava buried Pompeii, as in films. Many think the plaster figures are statues or real people turned to stone. Some think the town was always known, or mix up AD 79 with 1748. Ask: if lava had flowed in, would bread and paintings have survived? Be careful with the date: the eruption is often given as 24 August, but new evidence points to October, so AD 79 is safest."]},"familyTip":"Look at photos of Pompeii's streets, the stepping stones and the Beware of the Dog mosaic together. Ask: what does this tell us about the people who lived there?","keyIdea":"Pompeii was a busy Roman town in Italy near a mountain called Vesuvius. In AD 79 Vesuvius erupted. Lava did not reach the town: a giant cloud dropped pumice stones and ash on it for many hours, then clouds of red-hot ash and gas rushed over it and buried it 4 to 6 metres deep. The ash shut out air, rain and people, so it kept buildings, paintings, bread and even the shapes of people and animals, whose bodies left hollows that archaeologists later filled with plaster. The town lay hidden and forgotten for about 1,700 years until digging began in 1748. Because a whole town was frozen in time, its finds show how ordinary Romans lived.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"True about Pompeii or not? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True","Not true"],"items":[["Vesuvius erupted in AD 79",0],["Ash and pumice buried the town",0],["The ash kept bread and paintings",0],["People still dig there today",0],["Lava flowed over the town",1],["The town was never found again",1],["The plaster shapes are Roman statues",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpomp","caption":"Flagship lab: tap Vesuvius hour by hour, bury things in the ash, make a plaster cast, put the story on a timeline, dig up clues to Roman life.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uUa6cgv8zV":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which of these is made of rock?","choices":["A mountain","A tree","A cloud"],"answer":0,"hint":"One of them grows and one floats in the sky.","explain":"A mountain is a huge pile of rock. A tree is a living plant and a cloud is water drops."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where do pebbles come from?","choices":["Big rocks that broke up","Seeds in the ground","A factory"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the boulder you broke in the lab.","explain":"Pebbles are bits of bigger rocks. Rivers and waves roll them until they are smooth."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam has {a} pebbles. He finds {b} more on a path. How many rocks does he have now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Pebbles are small rocks, so add them all.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} pebbles, and every pebble is a rock.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is under the sea floor?","choices":["Rock","Air","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember the drill under the sea in the lab.","explain":"The outside of Earth is rock all the way round, under the land and under the sea."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia says rocks must be hard. What do you say?","choices":["Some rocks are soft, like chalk","Mia is right","Rocks are made of jelly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a soft rock that you can draw with.","explain":"Most rocks are hard, but chalk is a soft rock. Hard or soft, it is still rock."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You dig {n} spades of soil, then hit rock. You dig 1 more spade. How many spades in all?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Add the 1 extra spade to the spades of soil.","explain":"{n} + 1 = {=n+1} spades. Under the soil, the spade always hits rock.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which of these is not rock?","choices":["A leaf","A pebble","Sand"],"answer":0,"hint":"One of them grew on a tree.","explain":"A leaf grew on a tree, so it is not rock. Pebbles and sand are bits of rock."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What turns big rocks into sand?","choices":["Rain, ice and waves, over a long time","People with hammers, in one day","Rocks never change"],"answer":0,"hint":"The lab said this takes many, many years.","explain":"Rain, ice, rivers and waves wear rock away very slowly. In time, big rocks become sand."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You dig a deep hole in the garden. What do you reach under the soil?","choices":["Solid rock","An empty space","Soil that never ends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the spade hit at the bottom of the hole in the lab.","explain":"Under the soil there is always solid rock. Soil is only a thin top layer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You find {a} pebbles and {b} shells on the beach. How many pebbles are pieces of rock?","answer":"a","hint":"Pebbles are rock. Shells were made by sea animals, so do not count them.","explain":"All {a} pebbles are rock. The {b} shells are not rock, so the answer is {a}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is sand made of?","choices":["Tiny bits of rock","Sugar","Tiny seeds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Waves grind pebbles smaller and smaller. What is left?","explain":"Sand is tiny grains of rock. Waves and wind wear big rocks down into sand."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A big rock breaks into {n} stones. Then one stone breaks in 2. How many pieces now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"One stone turns into 2, so you have one more piece than before.","explain":"{n} stones, and one splits in 2, makes {=n+1} pieces. They are all still rock.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Chalk is soft. Is chalk a rock?","choices":["Yes, rocks can be soft","No, rocks are always hard","No, chalk is a plant"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, where did the chalk card go?","explain":"Chalk is a soft rock. Some rocks are hard and some are soft, but they are all rock."}],"explain":{"kid":["The ground under your feet is rock. On top there is grass and soil. Plants grow in the soil. If you dig down and down, you hit hard, solid rock.","You can see rock in lots of places. A mountain is a huge pile of rock. A cliff is a wall of rock. Pebbles are small rocks. Sand is tiny bits of rock.","Big rocks break into small bits. Rain, ice and waves wear them away. It takes a very long time. A big rock turns into stones, then pebbles, then sand.","Some rocks are hard. Some are soft, like chalk. They are all rock. Earth is like a giant ball. Its outside is rock all the way round, even under the sea."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. The idea that Earth is made of rock and other natural materials underpins the rocks units of the National Curriculum in England (Year 2 everyday materials, Year 3 rocks and soils) and NGSS 2-ESS1-1 and 4-ESS2-1 on slow changes and weathering by water, ice and wind. Children learn that soil is a thin top layer made partly of broken rock, that solid rock (bedrock) lies under it everywhere, and that sand and pebbles are pieces of rock.","The lab has five tasks. Children dig a hole through soil and stony soil to bedrock, find the rock in a landscape (mountain, cliff, beach sand, river pebbles) while a tree and a cloud are not, break a boulder into stones, pebbles and sand, sort seven cards into rock and not rock, and drill under a city, the sea and a desert. The outer layer of Earth, the crust, is solid rock all the way round, including under the oceans.","Common mistakes. Many children think soil goes down forever, that Earth is hollow, or that rocks must be hard, so they reject chalk and sand. Some think pebbles grow or are made by people. Take care with the deep Earth: the mantle is very hot rock and the outer core is liquid metal, so this lesson says the outside of Earth is rock, not that Earth is rock all the way to the centre."]},"familyTip":"Go on a rock hunt in a park, a beach or your street. Pick up a pebble, feel some sand and look at a wall or a step. For each one, ask: is this a bit of the Earth? Rub two pebbles together over paper and look at the tiny grains that fall off.","keyIdea":"The ground under your feet is rock. If you dig down through the soil, you always reach solid rock. Rock is all around us: mountains, cliffs, pebbles and even sand are rock. Big rocks slowly break into stones, pebbles and tiny grains of sand. Some rocks are hard and some are soft, like chalk, but they are all rock. The outside of Earth is rock all the way round, under the land and under the sea.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it rock? Drag each card, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Rock","Not rock"],"items":[["A cliff",0],["A feather",1],["Gravel",0],["A paper cup",1],["A boulder",0],["A puddle",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrock","caption":"Flagship lab: dig down to solid rock, find rock in a landscape, break a boulder into sand, sort rocks, and drill under a city and the sea.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_jwElFY7Syd":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which layer is at the very centre of Earth?","choices":["The inner core","The mantle","The crust"],"answer":0,"hint":"The core has two parts. Which part is in the middle?","explain":"The inner core is at the very centre. Around it is the outer core, then the mantle, then the crust."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which layer is liquid?","choices":["The outer core","The inner core","The crust"],"answer":0,"hint":"One part of the core flows. Is it the inner part or the outer part?","explain":"The outer core is liquid metal. The inner core is solid, because it is squeezed so hard."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where is Earth hottest?","choices":["At the centre","At the crust","Halfway down the mantle"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to the heat as you go deeper?","explain":"It gets hotter the deeper you go. The centre is about 5,000 °C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"The mantle is about 2,900 km thick. A drill has gone {d} km into the mantle. How many km of mantle are left?","answer":"2900-d","hint":"Take the distance drilled away from 2,900.","explain":"2,900 − {d} = {=2900-d} km of mantle are left. The mantle is very thick.","params":{"d":[100,900]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Name the layers from the outside in.","choices":["Crust, mantle, outer core, inner core","Mantle, crust, outer core, inner core","Crust, outer core, mantle, inner core"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with the layer we live on and end at the centre.","explain":"From the outside in: crust, mantle, outer core, inner core."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the core made of?","choices":["Metal, mostly iron","Rock and ice","Water"],"answer":0,"hint":"The crust and mantle are rock. The core is different.","explain":"The core is metal, mostly iron with some nickel. It is far too hot for ice or water."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why is the crust drawn thick in most pictures of Earth?","choices":["So you can see it","Because it is the thickest layer","Because it is hot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the true thickness bars. Would the real crust show up?","explain":"The real crust is so thin it would be a thin line. Pictures draw it thicker so you can see it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Leo says the inner core is liquid because it is the hottest part. What did he forget?","choices":["It is squeezed so hard it stays solid","It is cold","It is made of rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about all the layers pressing down on the centre.","explain":"The inner core is the hottest part, but the weight of all the layers squeezes it so hard that it stays solid metal."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"The crust under a town is about 35 km thick. A deep mine goes {d} km down. How many km of crust are still below the mine?","answer":"35-d","hint":"Take the depth of the mine away from 35 km.","explain":"35 − {d} = {=35-d} km of crust are still below. Even the deepest mines do not get through the crust.","params":{"d":[2,8]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which layer do we live on?","choices":["The crust","The mantle","The core"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which layer is on the outside of Earth?","explain":"We live on the crust, the thin rocky outside layer of Earth."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A drill goes down {a} km. Then it goes down {b} km more. How far down is it?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two distances to find how far down the drill has gone.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} km. The crust under land is about 35 km thick, so the drill is still in the crust if the answer is less than 35.","params":{"a":[3,12],"b":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which layer is the thinnest?","choices":["The crust","The mantle","The outer core"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the bars in the lab. Which one was so small you could hardly see it?","explain":"The crust is the thinnest layer, about 35 km. The mantle is about 2,900 km thick."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ben draws the crust {c} mm thick. The mantle is about 80 times as thick as the crust. How thick should he draw the mantle, in mm?","answer":"80*c","hint":"Multiply the crust by 80.","explain":"{c} × 80 = {=80*c} mm. That is why real-size drawings make the crust look like a thin line.","params":{"c":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Mia says the mantle is a sea of runny lava. Is she right?","choices":["No, it is mostly solid hot rock","Yes, it is all liquid","No, it is cold ice"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, did the mantle go in the solid box or the liquid box?","explain":"The mantle is mostly solid rock. It is very hot and creeps very slowly, but it is not a sea of lava."}],"explain":{"kid":["Earth is made of layers, like a boiled egg. The outside layer is the crust. It is the rock we live on, under the land and under the sea.","Under the crust is the mantle. It is a very thick layer of hot rock. The rock is mostly solid, but it is so hot that it creeps very, very slowly.","At the centre is the core. It is made of metal, mostly iron. The outer core is liquid metal. The inner core is solid metal, because it is squeezed so hard.","The crust is the thinnest layer by far. If Earth were an apple, the crust would be as thin as its skin. The deeper you go, the hotter it gets. The centre is hottest of all."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. This supports the National Curriculum in England (Year 3 rocks; Year 4–6 geography on volcanoes and earthquakes) and NGSS 4-ESS2 and MS-ESS2-1 on Earth's materials. Rounded values from the USGS and BGS: Earth's radius is about 6,371 km, the crust is about 5–10 km thick under oceans and 30–70 km under continents, the mantle about 2,900 km, the outer core about 2,200 km and the inner core about 1,220 km in radius.","The lab has five tasks. Children drill from the surface to the centre and meet each layer, place the four names on a cut-away drawing (with the crust drawn thicker so it shows), order the layers from thinnest to thickest using true sizes, order them from coolest to hottest, sort them as solid or liquid, and check three children's statements.","Common mistakes. Many children picture the mantle as a sea of liquid lava; it is mostly solid rock that flows only over millions of years, and magma forms in a few places near the top. Others think the crust is the thickest layer, because diagrams draw it thick, or that the core is cold or hollow. Watch, too, for the slip that the hottest inner core must be liquid: pressure keeps it solid."]},"familyTip":"Cut a hard-boiled egg in half, shell on. The shell is the crust, the white is the mantle and the yolk is the core. Ask: which part is thinnest? Then look at a peach or a plum: the skin, the flesh and the stone make the same three layers.","keyIdea":"Earth is made of layers, like a peach or a boiled egg. The outside layer is the crust, the rock we live on. It is very thin: about 35 km under the land, which is thinner than the skin on an apple if Earth were apple-sized. Under it is the mantle, about 2,900 km of very hot rock that is mostly solid but creeps very slowly. At the centre is the core, made of metal. The outer core is liquid metal and the inner core is solid metal. The deeper you go, the hotter it gets: the centre is about 5,000 °C.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which layer is it? Drag each card, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Crust","Mantle","Core"],"items":[["The rock we live on",0],["Liquid metal and solid metal",2],["The thickest layer of rock",1],["Under the oceans and the land",0],["The very centre of Earth",2],["Hot rock that creeps slowly",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglayers","caption":"Flagship lab: drill to Earth's centre, name the four layers, order them by thickness and by heat, sort solid and liquid layers, and check facts.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Wu-ftkzoiE":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is 'tidal wave' the wrong name for a tsunami?","choices":["Tides do not cause tsunamis","Tsunamis are too small","Tsunamis only happen at night"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what really starts a tsunami.","explain":"Tsunamis come from earthquakes, landslides or volcanoes moving the water, not from tides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How tall is a tsunami out in the deep ocean?","choices":["Often under 1 m","As tall as a tower","It is a wall of water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do ships out at sea notice it?","explain":"In the deep ocean a tsunami is often under 1 m tall, so ships hardly notice. It grows near land."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why does a tsunami slow down near the beach?","choices":["The water gets shallow","The wind stops","The tide pulls it back"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, what changed as the wave came close to land?","explain":"Tsunami speed depends on the depth of the sea. In shallow water it slows down and grows taller."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The first tsunami wave has gone back out. What now?","choices":["Stay on high ground","Go back to the beach","Go and help boats in the harbour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a tsunami one wave or many?","explain":"A tsunami is a series of waves for hours, and a later one can be the biggest. Stay up high until it is called safe."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which of these is a natural warning of a tsunami?","choices":["The ground shakes hard near the sea","A sunny morning","Small waves on a windy day"],"answer":0,"hint":"What usually starts a tsunami?","explain":"Strong shaking near the coast can mean an undersea earthquake. Go to high ground."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which can NOT start a tsunami?","choices":["Strong wind","An underwater landslide","A volcano erupting into the sea"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one only moves the top of the sea?","explain":"Wind only makes normal waves on the surface. Landslides and volcanoes can push the whole sea."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Where is a tsunami fastest?","choices":["In the deep ocean","Near the beach","In a river"],"answer":0,"hint":"The deeper the water, the faster the wave.","explain":"In the deep ocean a tsunami goes about 700 km/h, like a jet plane."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Near the beach a tsunami moves {s} km/h. You can run {r} km/h. How much faster is the wave, in km/h?","answer":"s-r","params":{"s":[30,40],"r":[10,15]},"hint":"Take your running speed away from the wave's speed.","explain":"{s} − {r} = {=s-r} km/h faster. You cannot outrun it on the beach, so go to high ground as soon as there is a warning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A tsunami crosses the ocean at {s} km/h for {h} hours. How far does it travel, in km?","answer":"s*h","params":{"s":[500,700],"h":[3,6]},"hint":"Multiply the speed by the number of hours.","explain":"{s} × {h} = {=s*h} km. Warning centres use this to tell faraway coasts when to expect the waves."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What starts most tsunamis?","choices":["An earthquake under the sea","Strong wind","The tide"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can push all of the sea water at once, from the floor up?","explain":"Most tsunamis start with an undersea earthquake that lifts or drops the sea floor. Wind and tides cannot start one."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A tsunami travels {s} km/h across the deep ocean. How far does it go in {h} hours, in km?","answer":"s*h","params":{"s":[600,800],"h":[2,5]},"hint":"Distance is speed times time. Multiply the speed by the hours.","explain":"{s} km/h × {h} hours = {=s*h} km. That is why a tsunami can cross an ocean in less than a day."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Where is a tsunami tallest?","choices":["Near the beach","In the deep ocean","In the middle of the sea"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, where did the wave grow when it slowed down?","explain":"Near the beach the water is shallow, so the wave slows down and piles up tall. In the deep ocean it is low."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The first wave arrives at {a} o'clock. Another wave comes {b} hours later. At what o'clock does it arrive?","answer":"a+b","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,4]},"hint":"Add the hours to the time of the first wave.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} o'clock. Tsunami waves keep coming for hours, so stay on high ground."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"The sea suddenly rushes far out. What should you do?","choices":["Run to high ground","Walk out to look","Collect the shells"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the sea going out a good sign or a warning?","explain":"The sea rushing out is a warning that a tsunami may be coming. Go to high ground at once."}],"explain":{"kid":["A tsunami is a set of huge waves. It starts when a lot of sea water is pushed all at once. Most tsunamis start with an earthquake under the sea, when a piece of sea floor jumps up or drops down.","Out in the deep ocean, the waves are low. They may be less than 1 m tall, so ships hardly notice. But they move as fast as a jet plane.","Near the land the sea gets shallow. The waves slow down, but they pile up and grow very tall. They can flood far onto the land.","Nature can warn you. The ground shakes hard, the sea rushes far out, or you hear a roar from the sea. Go to high ground at once. Stay there, because more waves can come for hours."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. This supports the National Curriculum in England (geography: describe and understand earthquakes and volcanoes, Key Stage 2) and NGSS 4-ESS3-2 (solutions to reduce the impacts of natural Earth processes on humans). Facts follow NOAA's Tsunami Program, the USGS and the UNESCO IOC Tsunami Glossary: wave speed is about the square root of gravity times depth, so about 700 km/h in 4,000 m of water, about 110 km/h in 100 m and about 35 km/h in 10 m.","The lab has six tasks. Children sort what can and cannot start a tsunami, push a block of sea floor up and watch the water above lift and spread as two waves, tap three places from the fastest wave to the slowest, sort natural warning signs, choose what to do in three moments, and judge three classmates.","Common mistakes. Many children call a tsunami a 'tidal wave' and think tides or wind cause it, picture it as a giant wall of water out at sea, or think it is a single wave, so it is safe after the first one. A dangerous slip is going down to the beach to look when the sea draws back. Stress: go high at once, and stay until officials say it is safe."]},"familyTip":"Fill a long baking tray with a little water. Lift one end of a ruler lying flat on the bottom with a quick push and watch the bump race to the far end. Then talk about a family plan: if you are ever at the coast and the ground shakes hard, where is the nearest high ground?","keyIdea":"A tsunami is a series of huge waves made when a lot of sea water is pushed all at once, most often by an earthquake under the sea. Landslides and volcanoes can start one too, but wind and tides cannot. In the deep ocean the waves are low, often under 1 m, but as fast as a jet plane. Near land the sea gets shallow, so the waves slow down and grow tall. Strong shaking, the sea rushing far out or a loud roar are warnings: go to high ground at once and stay there, because more waves come.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it true about tsunamis? Drag each card, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True","Not true"],"items":[["Most start with an earthquake under the sea",0],["They are made by the tide",1],["They are fastest in deep water",0],["The first wave is always the biggest",1],["They grow tall near the beach",0],["Strong wind makes them",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtsun","caption":"Flagship lab: sort what starts a tsunami, push the sea floor, find where waves are fastest, spot warning signs and choose what to do.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
